"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[47156],{147156:e=>{e.exports="# Snowflake Scripting — Procedures, UDFs, and Variables\n\nSnowflake stored procedures, UDFs, and variable declarations → DBSQL equivalents. See `05b-scripting-control-flow.md` for control flow, cursors, error handling, and dynamic SQL.\n\n> For DBSQL stored procedure syntax, load `skills/writing-sql/stored-procedures.md`.\n> For DBSQL scripting syntax, load `skills/writing-sql/sql-scripting.md`.\n> For DBSQL dynamic SQL, load `skills/writing-sql/dynamic-sql.md`.\n\n> **Scripting context vs. stored-procedure context**: top-level scripting (in a notebook cell) places `DECLARE` outside any `BEGIN ... END`. Inside a stored procedure body, `DECLARE` must appear at the top of the procedure's `BEGIN ... END`, before any executable statement. Placing `DECLARE` before the procedure's `AS BEGIN` causes `PARSE_SYNTAX_ERROR`.\n\n## SNOWFLAKE\n\n### Stored procedures (Snowflake Scripting, LANGUAGE SQL)\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake Scripting\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE my_db.my_schema.my_proc(param1 INT, param2 VARCHAR DEFAULT 'default')\nRETURNS INT\nLANGUAGE SQL\nAS\n$$\nDECLARE\n  result INT;\nBEGIN\n  result := param1 * 2;\n  RETURN result;\nEND;\n$$;\n\n-- DBSQL (scalar return via OUT parameter; SQL SECURITY INVOKER is required)\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE my_catalog.my_schema.my_proc(\n  IN param1 INT,\n  IN param2 STRING DEFAULT 'default',\n  OUT result INT\n)\nLANGUAGE SQL\nSQL SECURITY INVOKER\nAS BEGIN\n  DECLARE tmp INT;\n  SET tmp = param1 * 2;\n  SET result = tmp;\nEND;\n```\n\n**Critical differences:**\n\n- **Scalar RETURN is not supported in DBSQL procedures.** Snowflake procedures return scalars via `RETURN expr`; DBSQL procedures have no scalar return — use `OUT` parameters or restructure as a UDF. For scalar-returning Snowflake procs:\n  1. If the logic is expressible as a pure function, **convert to a DBSQL UDF** (see UDF section below).\n  2. Otherwise, add an `OUT` parameter and rewrite the call site to read the output.\n- **Tabular results**: DBSQL procedures return the **last executed `SELECT`'s result set** to the caller. The public DBSQL `CREATE PROCEDURE` syntax does not document a `RETURNS TABLE(...)` clause — that clause is for `CREATE FUNCTION` (table-valued UDF). For Snowflake procs that return a result set, either (a) place a trailing `SELECT` in the body, or (b) convert to a table-valued function.\n- `$$...$$` dollar-quoted body → drop the delimiters; the procedure body is a plain `BEGIN ... END` in DBSQL.\n- Snowflake `RETURNS <type>` for scalar → no clause in DBSQL (scalar return unsupported on procedures; see options above).\n- Snowflake `EXECUTE AS CALLER` / `EXECUTE AS OWNER` → DBSQL supports only `SQL SECURITY INVOKER` (= EXECUTE AS CALLER semantics). If the Snowflake procedure relied on OWNER-rights access to objects the caller cannot see, flag this in a migration comment.\n- Snowflake variable assignment `var := expr;` → DBSQL `SET var = expr;`.\n\n**Security clause (`SQL SECURITY INVOKER`)**: always include it — omitting it raises `MISSING_CLAUSES_FOR_OPERATION: SQL SECURITY` (SQLSTATE 42601). It's the only accepted value in DBSQL.\n\n**BEGIN vs BEGIN ATOMIC**: the procedure body is a compound statement — either `BEGIN ... END` or `BEGIN ATOMIC ... END`. `ATOMIC` adds automatic rollback on error for the statements in the block; on DBR 17.0+ it works for single-table transactions, and DBR 18.0+ / SQL warehouse / serverless for multi-table atomicity. A bare `BEGIN` works everywhere SQL scripting is available. Use `ATOMIC` when the procedure touches multiple DML statements that must succeed together.\n\n```sql\n-- DML-only procedure (notebook / classic)\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE my_catalog.my_schema.proc_name(IN p1 INT)\nLANGUAGE SQL\nSQL SECURITY INVOKER\nBEGIN\n  INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t SELECT p1;\n  UPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.t SET status = 'done' WHERE id = p1;\nEND;\n\n-- Procedure that returns a result set: the LAST executed SELECT is returned to the caller.\n-- Do NOT add a RETURNS TABLE clause on CREATE PROCEDURE — it's not a documented syntax element.\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE my_catalog.my_schema.proc_name(IN p1 INT)\nLANGUAGE SQL\nSQL SECURITY INVOKER\nBEGIN\n  SELECT col1, col2 FROM my_catalog.my_schema.t WHERE id = p1;\nEND;\n```\n\nFor strongly-typed tabular return, convert the Snowflake procedure to a DBSQL **table-valued function** (see UDF section below) — `RETURNS TABLE(...)` is valid on `CREATE FUNCTION`, not `CREATE PROCEDURE`.\n\n### Calling procedures\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCALL my_db.my_schema.my_proc(10);\n\n-- DBSQL (with OUT parameter — session-level call from a notebook cell)\nDECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE result INT;\nCALL my_catalog.my_schema.my_proc(10, 'default', result);\nSELECT result;\n```\n\n### Multi-result-set procedures\n\nDBSQL procedures return only the **last** `SELECT` to the caller. If the Snowflake source returns multiple result sets (via multiple SELECTs in a body, or via `RESULTSET` manipulation), consolidate before writing:\n\n- **Option A — UNION ALL with discriminator** (preferred when schemas are compatible):\n  ```sql\n  -- NOTE: Snowflake returned 2 result sets. Combined with UNION ALL + result_set discriminator.\n  SELECT 'set1' AS result_set, col1, col2 FROM t1\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT 'set2' AS result_set, col1, col2 FROM t2\n  ```\n- **Option B — stage in temp tables, emit the final result**:\n  ```sql\n  CREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY VIEW stage1 AS SELECT ... ;\n  CREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY VIEW stage2 AS SELECT ... ;\n  -- single final SELECT returns to the caller\n  SELECT * FROM stage1 UNION ALL SELECT * FROM stage2;\n  ```\n- **Option C — discard and document** (last resort, only when schemas are genuinely incompatible):\n  ```sql\n  -- NOTE: Snowflake returned N result sets. DBSQL procedures return only the last SELECT.\n  -- Returning <which set and why>. Discarded sets: <list>.\n  ```\n\n### Non-SQL procedure languages\n\nSnowflake supports procedure bodies in JavaScript, Python, Java, and Scala. Migration options depend on the language.\n\n**`LANGUAGE JAVASCRIPT`** — widely used in Snowflake. DBSQL has no JavaScript runtime.\n\nOptions:\n\n1. **Rewrite in Snowflake Scripting SQL first** (on Snowflake side), then migrate to DBSQL SQL procedure.\n2. **Rewrite as a SQL UDF or Python UDF** in DBSQL.\n3. Mark as TODO with an explicit strategy note.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake JS procedure\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE clean_name(name VARCHAR)\nRETURNS VARCHAR LANGUAGE JAVASCRIPT AS\n$$\n  return NAME.trim().toLowerCase();\n$$;\n\n-- DBSQL option 1: SQL UDF (if the logic is pure)\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.clean_name(name STRING)\nRETURNS STRING\nRETURN LOWER(TRIM(name));\n\n-- DBSQL option 2: Python UDF (for complex logic)\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.clean_name(name STRING)\nRETURNS STRING\nLANGUAGE PYTHON\nAS $$\n  return name.strip().lower() if name else None\n$$;\n```\n\n**`LANGUAGE PYTHON`** — Snowflake Python stored procedures (Snowpark). Databricks supports Python UDFs via `CREATE FUNCTION ... LANGUAGE PYTHON`. Note: **Snowpark DataFrame code is not directly portable** to DBSQL — Snowpark and PySpark DataFrame APIs differ. Flag any `session.table(...)`, Snowpark joins, or `snowflake.snowpark.*` imports as manual rewrite.\n\n**`LANGUAGE JAVA` / `LANGUAGE SCALA`** — Databricks supports Scala/Java UDFs via JAR registration. Body shape differs — flag for manual review.\n\n### User-Defined Functions (UDFs)\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake scalar SQL UDF\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION get_full_name(first VARCHAR, last VARCHAR)\nRETURNS VARCHAR\nAS $$\n  first || ' ' || last\n$$;\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.get_full_name(first STRING, last STRING)\nRETURNS STRING\nRETURN CONCAT(first, ' ', last);\n```\n\n**Table-valued UDFs**: Snowflake `RETURNS TABLE(...)` table functions have DBSQL equivalents.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION employees_by_dept(dept_id INT)\nRETURNS TABLE (id INT, name VARCHAR)\nAS\n$$\n  SELECT id, name FROM employees WHERE department_id = dept_id\n$$;\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.employees_by_dept(dept_id INT)\nRETURNS TABLE (id INT, name STRING)\nRETURN SELECT id, name FROM my_catalog.my_schema.employees WHERE department_id = dept_id;\n```\n\n### Variables\n\nThree Snowflake variable contexts; each maps to a different DBSQL pattern.\n\n**1. Snowflake Scripting (block-level, inside `BEGIN ... END`):**\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake Scripting\nDECLARE\n  myvar INT DEFAULT 10;\nBEGIN\n  LET myvar2 INT := 20;          -- LET with inferred type\n  myvar := myvar + myvar2;       -- assignment\n  ...\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL — `DECLARE` at top of the same compound statement\nBEGIN\n  DECLARE myvar INT DEFAULT 10;\n  DECLARE myvar2 INT DEFAULT 20;\n  SET myvar = myvar + myvar2;\n  ...\nEND;\n```\n\n**2. Snowflake top-of-file / session-level — single-var `SET` (CRITICAL):**\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake (top of file, outside any procedure)\nSET myvar = 10;\nSELECT $myvar;\n\n-- DBSQL — DECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE at session/notebook scope; reference by bare name\nDECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE myvar INT DEFAULT 10;\nSELECT myvar;\n```\n\n`DECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE` is the documented DBSQL session-variable form. It does **not** require a `BEGIN ... END` wrapper, so it preserves the top-level shape of the original Snowflake script (multiple top-level DML statements that all read the same variable).\n\n**3. Snowflake top-of-file — tuple `SET (a, b) = (val_a, val_b)`:**\n\nDBSQL has no tuple-`DECLARE`. Split into one statement per variable.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nSET (sdate, edate) = (current_date - 2, current_date - 1);\nDELETE FROM t WHERE dt BETWEEN $sdate AND $edate;\nINSERT INTO t SELECT * FROM src WHERE dt BETWEEN $sdate AND $edate;\n\n-- DBSQL\nDECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE sdate DATE = current_date - 2;\nDECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE edate DATE = current_date - 1;\nDELETE FROM my_catalog.my_schema.t WHERE dt BETWEEN sdate AND edate;\nINSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t SELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.src WHERE dt BETWEEN sdate AND edate;\n```\n\nAlways pick an explicit DBSQL type (`DATE`, `INT`, `STRING`, `TIMESTAMP_NTZ`, `DECIMAL(p,s)`, …) for each variable. If the Snowflake source relied on Snowflake's inference, infer from the right-hand-side expression.\n\n**`$var` references**: strip the `$` and use the bare variable name (`$sdate` → `sdate`). Same rule applies inside procedure bodies for `:param` colon-bind references — strip `:` and use the bare name.\n\n**Variable assignment edge cases:**\n\n- If a `SELECT ... INTO var` returns **0 rows**, the variable retains its default (same as Snowflake).\n- If the `SELECT` returns **multiple rows**, DBSQL raises an error. Snowflake Scripting also raises. Safe migration — no behavior change.\n- To read multiple columns into multiple variables:\n  ```sql\n  -- DBSQL (inside a compound statement / procedure body)\n  SET (a, b) = (SELECT col1, col2 FROM t WHERE id = 1);\n  ```\n"}}]);
